CORE
main.cpp
--cpp20
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ span size: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ span element: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^.*is unknown.*$
^CONVERSION ERROR$
^.*does not uniquely resolve.*$
--
Genuine (non-vacuous) verification of a populated std::span<int>.

This previously passed vacuously (std::span construction failed to elaborate
its iterator, truncating main and dropping the assertions), then -- once the
iterator's inert C++20 `iterator_concept` alias was kept lazy
([iterator.concepts.general], [temp.inst]/1-2) -- ran genuinely but FAILED:
`span size` held but `span element` did not, because span(pointer, size_type)'s
body sets `_M_ptr = std::to_address(__first)` and std::to_address's call to
__to_address(_Tp*) tied with __to_address(const _Ptr&) for the pointer
argument.

It now verifies because overload resolution applies N5008 [temp.func.order]
partial ordering (function_template_at_least_as_specialised): __to_address(_Tp*)
is more specialized than __to_address(const _Ptr&), so to_address returns the
supplied pointer and _M_ptr is exact.  Both size() and element access are
checked.
